{
  "name": "happy",
  "integration": "runzero-task-sync",
  "script": "runzero-task-sync/runzero-task-sync.star",
  "description": "The full mirror of two tasks from one runZero instance to another, which is a five-hop chain per task and is only observable through the requests it makes -- the script imports nothing itself, it hands scan data to the destination console's own import endpoint. Task listing uses the real GET /api/v1.0/org/tasks shape: a bare array of models.TaskWithJoin (no envelope, no pagination), with the join-only fields site_name/agent_name/template_name that the write endpoints do not return. The download hop is the part worth the fixture: /org/tasks/<id>/data does NOT return the bytes, it answers 303 See Other with a Location pointing at a presigned object-store URL and a {\"url\": ...} body, so the run only works if the HTTP client follows the redirect -- two requests reach the server per download, and asserting on the object path proves the second one happened. What comes back is a real gzip member, served via body_base64 because a `text` body is UTF-8 encoded on the way out and would mangle every byte above 0x7f, the 0x8b of the gzip magic included. The scan data itself is opaque to this integration: it decompresses and recompresses without ever parsing it, so the payload's internal shape is not part of the contract under test. That is also why 'Successfully synced task' is the load-bearing log assertion -- it is printed only after gzip_decompress, gzip_compress, and a 200 from the destination import, so it is transitive proof the whole binary round trip worked. hide_tasks_on_sync is on, so each synced task is then hidden at the SOURCE with the source org id -- pinning that the hide call goes to src_url and not to dst_url, which would hide a task on the wrong console.",

